From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Faust <willtechnoduck@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 12:23:48 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Add a loader-safe image-registration export, and recognise
 wine-mono opt-in

NotifyImageMap() exists because wine's loader must be able to register a
module's executable intervals from a thread that is executing translated code,
and NotifyMapViewOfSection() cannot be called from there. That path drives
ImageTracker::HandleImageMap(), which opens with an EXCLUSIVE scoped_lock on
CodeInvalidationMutex; a thread running translated code already holds that
mutex SHARED, there is no read-to-write upgrade, and it blocks on itself
forever while holding wine's loader lock. Book of the Dead froze that way on a
module unload and Marvel Cosmic Invasion on cryptbase.dll's load.

So this touches only InvalidationTracker, which takes its own IntervalsLock.
That is the half that matters for correctness: the intervals are what gate
decoding, and without them the first x86-64 instruction in an unregistered
module raises NoExecOp and takes the GuestSignal_SIGSEGV trampoline.

What it deliberately does NOT do is the ImageTracker half. That is a real gap,
not merely skipped bookkeeping: ImageTracker owns image relocation and
code-cache information plus extended volatile/ForceTSO metadata, and that
metadata stays incomplete for any module whose syscall notification was
genuinely missed. The durable fix is to repair the pseudo-process notification
gate, or to defer the ImageTracker half to a dispatcher point where no shared
code-invalidation hold exists. Neither is done here.

Separately, Mono detection now recognises wine-mono, which ships its runtime
as libmono-2.0-x86_64.dll and so matched neither existing name -- leaving the
hooks inert while wine-mono startup paid the full W^X tax.

Activation is OPT-IN (MYTHIC_WINEMONO_BRIDGE=1, default off) for a correctness
reason, not caution: the bridge reclassifies a detected XCHG from a true
atomic exchange into an alias-directed plain write, and deciding that by
filename alone would let any unrelated lock-free XCHG in that DLL lose its
atomicity -- wine-mono's thread-suspend machinery is exactly such code. The
two pre-existing names are Unity's embedded Mono, field-proven since ml648.

[mono-site] prints the first detected RIP, its module-relative RVA and the
instruction bytes before marking anything, because that is the only evidence
available for judging whether a site is a genuine patching routine.

Measured afterwards, the opt-in cannot help this particular storm: the hot
faulting instruction is inside UCRT memcpy, called from
mono_breakpoint_clean_code+0x68 via an import thunk, and
DetectMonoBackpatcherBlock requires the RIP to lie inside Mono's own range. It
stays off, and the storm remains a startup cost worth attacking by another
route.

